{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' docs/design/datom.world.md && sed -n '3520,3650p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md && sed -n '3930,3978p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md && rg -n \\\"yang.call|callable|php/call|not-callable|invalid-invocation\\\" php test* src\"","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n**must**, **must not**, and **may** state requirements on a future\nconforming implementation, not claims about current code.\n\n#### 8.10.1 Decision and scope\n\nA Yin function is a Yin function, independent of its source syntax. The\nVM applies a closure to operands. Source calling semantics are ordinary\nUAST implementing the call site and callee entry, above the VM.\n\nEvery supported source-level callable, after its source-required\nresolution, exposes `:yang.call/entry`, an ordinary Yin closure taking\nexactly one invocation record. Invoking it is an ordinary\n`:application`. No AST tag, lambda slot, instruction, host primitive,\neffect kind, or source-language test is added to the VM.\n\nThis is an internal calling convention, not a transport, another system\nABI, or a host callback interface. Calls between source languages inside\none task do not themselves cross a host boundary. Portable I/O and\ncross-task interaction continue through `dao.stream`.\n\n**Transparent calling** means that an admitted callable can be passed,\nstored, returned, and invoked by any conforming frontend without a\nhandwritten language-pair wrapper. It does not mean every foreign value\nsatisfies every source operation. Invocation, name visibility, and value\ninterpretation are distinct obligations.\n\nThe convention covers ordinary source calls and supported callable\nobjects, including higher-order calls. It does not make macros runtime\nfunctions, grant JavaScript `[[Construct]]`, turn a generator into an\nordinary returning function, or add a source feature a frontend refuses.\nConstructors and asynchronous operations keep their declared source\nsemantics; ordinary callability does not imply either capability.\n\n#### 8.10.2 Callable representation and identity\n\nKeep the callable's existing identity and source-specific data. Add the\ncommon entry beside its private code and metadata:\n\n```clojure\n;; Illustrative contents of one task-owned Python function cell.\n;; `entry` and `body-code` are live Yin closures, not literal AST maps.\n{:yang.call/entry entry\n :py/type :function\n :spec parameter-spec\n :defaults defaults\n :code body-code}\n```\n\nThe existing `:code` field is not the shared entry: its signature differs\nbetween preludes. It may remain private. Cross-language callers must not\ninspect it or extract it to bypass the entry.\n\nA cell-backed callable stores the entry in its cell contents. An\nimmutable callable descriptor, such as a bound method, may carry it\ndirectly in its map. The common prelude projects the entry structurally:\nread an existing sealed cell reference through `cell/get`, or inspect\nthe immutable descriptor, then obtain `:yang.call/entry`. It must not\ndispatch on `:py/type`, `:js/type`, `:php/type`, language names, or a\nregistry of languages.\n\nAn admitted callable is a runtime cell/descriptor constructed or exposed\nby the callable constructors or import/export projections of a pinned,\nconforming profile. The entry slot is a runtime descriptor field, not a\nsource property that promotes arbitrary data to a function. A Python\ndictionary, JavaScript object, or PHP array containing a source key\nspelled `:yang.call/entry` remains ordinary source data unless an explicit\nprofile-defined projection makes it a callable descriptor. Source\nproperty access must not overwrite or masquerade as this runtime slot.\nThis is an obligation of construction and source resolution, not a new\nVM type, descriptor seal, or runtime provenance/language registry. Once\nthe resolved descriptor is admitted, the shared projection is structural;\nUAST deliberately constructing a conforming descriptor opts into the\nconvention rather than changing a source language's data-map semantics.\n\nMissing entry means not callable. Constructors must install a genuine\nunary Yin closure, and supported source mutations must preserve that\ninvariant. Unary arity is a constructor/profile conformance obligation,\nnot a signature introspection operation: Yin closures are opaque and the\nVM's existing argument binding does not enforce this convention's arity.\nA key in a map grants no VM privilege; application, ownership, and\neffect checks still apply. A non-applicable entry remains an operational\nVM application defect, not a source exception caught by the shared helper.\n\nFor callable kinds whose eligibility can change, the same cell or\ndescriptor may also contain `:yang.call/eligible?`, a unary Yin predicate\non the original callable. Without it, an entry is eligible by default.\nWith it, the common callable test uses its boolean result. The predicate\nreads explicit runtime metadata/heap only; it must not mutate state,\nevaluate arguments, execute the target body, or invoke getters/user hooks.\nIt has the same constructor/arity obligations as the entry. For example,\nan instance can keep one dispatch entry while eligibility follows whether\nits class currently supplies a supported call method; changing the class\ndoes not require rewriting every instance.\n\nCall eligibility is tested at the callability-check point required by\nthe caller's source, not universally before argument evaluation. The\nentry resolves/rechecks a dynamic target at its source-required dispatch\npoint so argument side effects that change eligibility remain visible.\nStatic callable kinds need no predicate.\n\nSource callable designators, such as PHP function-name strings and\ncallable arrays where supported, are resolved under their source's rules\nbefore common invocation. They do not gain entry slots merely by being\nstrings or arrays. Exporting a designator as a first-class callable needs\nan explicit frontend projection to an admitted descriptor, retaining any\nsource-required late lookup and receiver semantics. Passing the raw\nstring or array as data leaves it unchanged; a foreign caller must not\nguess its language or search an ambient namespace to invoke it. This is\nsource resolution/projection, not a language-pair wrapper around an\nalready admitted function.\n\nCross-language passage must not allocate a replacement function cell.\nReturned functions retain their original identity, captures, defaults,\nbound receiver, properties, and static state. A receiving frontend's\ncallable test follows this common eligibility rule rather than rejecting\nforeign native tags. Foreign attribute access and type reflection remain\nprofile-owned; callability grants no general right to interpret another\nruntime's cell layout.\n\nCompiler-internal lambdas and host functions do not acquire this\nconvention merely by being applicable in Yin. A source import exposing\none must emit an entry from its declared signature and effect profile.\nIt must not guess an opaque closure's signature or bypass host-effect\nand custody rules. Internal helper calls need not construct records.\n\n#### 8.10.3 Invocation record\n\nThe invocation is an ephemeral in-machine value. These keys are proposed\nvocabulary, not existing repository APIs:\n\n```clojure\nsupported: a claiming profile must define and test restoration and\nreference lifetimes for every crossed frame.\n\nInvoking a function-valued argument is internal Yin computation.\nHost-initiated callbacks remain stream events under\n`datom.world.abi.md`; this convention adds no host callback facility.\n\n#### 8.10.9 Publication, visibility, and reproducibility\n\nShared callability creates no common global scope. An import or\ncomposition explicitly binds exports in the receiving source namespace\nunder its normal name/module rules. Missing names keep their source\nerrors. No fallback searches other frontends, the filesystem, a host\npackage registry, or ambient globals.\n\nLinked modules publish code and immutable specifications. Their bodies\ninstantiate function objects and common entries in the importing task,\nas section 8.5.6 requires. Install children must not export live function\ncells. A higher-order result is an ordinary runtime value, not a new\nlinker publication on each call.\n\nLowering/runtime profiles pin the implementation and binding/value\nextensions. Existing dependency/effect/custody checks remain. Type facts\nstay beside code under `yin.vm.typecheck`, not in lambda language tags.\nDo not duplicate existing lambda parameters in another persisted\nauthority over the private body signature.\n\nBefore admitting a compiled composition, its profile/dependency\ndeclarations must establish one compatible convention revision for the\nentries it exposes. An incompatible revision needs an explicitly pinned\nprojection or is refused before execution. Neither a common slot name nor\nan opaque runtime closure proves revision compatibility. No runtime\nlanguage registry or inspection of captured environments supplies it.\n\nChanged entry code changes code identities through the normal encoder.\nRetain source, lowered UAST, pinned profiles, and projections under\nexisting provenance/unitarity rules. A fast path may omit the record\nonly after proving equivalent evaluation, binding, foreign callability,\nexceptions, effects, suspension, and identity. Correctness must not\ndepend on that optimization.\n\n#### 8.10.10 Current code and migration\n\nThe current code records the gap, not a second normative convention:\n\n| Current implementation | Private call path |\n|---|---|\n| `yang.python.antlr.prelude` | `py/call-kw` binds arguments and applies the function cell's `:code` to one vector. |\n| `yang.typescript.antlr.prelude` | `js/call` checks `:js/type :function` and applies `:code` to receiver and argument vector. |\nrg: php: No such file or directory (os error 2)\nsrc/dev/jolt_profile_compare.cljc:434:  (prn [:case \"number/->Rational 1 2 callable\" (run-case #(number/ratio? (number/->Rational 1 2)))]))\ntest/dao/stream_test.cljc:385:    (testing \"descriptor is universal: callable on all handles regardless of surface\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:85:   callable-effects ; primitive effect index (D4), vm/primitive-effects-index\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:159:                     (:callable-effects vm))\ntest/yang/php/antlr/programs_p3b.cljc:497:    :note \"C28: PHP stops at the send of z (side() never runs); here php/call-named validates at entry, so side() runs first\"}\ntest/yang/php/antlr/prelude_test.cljc:72:                                (if (< (data/count as) 1) :php/null (php/call me []))))\ntest/yang/php/antlr/prelude_test.cljc:74:                  (do (php/call f []) (php/call f []) (php/call f []) (php/call f [0]))))\ntest/yang/php/antlr/e2e_test.clj:120:  (testing \"unmodified PHP source calls a published TS global export through yang.call\"\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:259:   \"<?php\\nclass test_class {\\n   private function test_func1() {\\n     echo \\\"test_func1\\\\n\\\";\\n   }\\n   protected function test_func2() {\\n     echo \\\"test_func2\\\\n\\\";\\n   }\\n   static private function test_func3() {\\n     echo \\\"test_func3\\\\n\\\";\\n   }\\n   static protected function test_func4() {\\n     echo \\\"test_func4\\\\n\\\";\\n   }\\n   function test() {\\n     if (is_callable(array($this,'test_func1'))) {\\n         $this->test_func1();\\n     } else {\\n       echo \\\"test_func1 isn't callable from inside\\\\n\\\";\\n     }\\n     if (is_callable(array($this,'test_func2'))) {\\n         $this->test_func2();\\n     } else {\\n       echo \\\"test_func2 isn't callable from inside\\\\n\\\";\\n     }\\n     if (is_callable(array('test_class','test_func3'))) {\\n         test_class::test_func3();\\n     } else {\\n       echo \\\"test_func3 isn't callable from inside\\\\n\\\";\\n     }\\n     if (is_callable(array('test_class','test_func4'))) {\\n         test_class::test_func4();\\n     } else {\\n       echo \\\"test_func4 isn't callable from inside\\\\n\\\";\\n     }\\n   }\\n}\\n\\nclass foo extends test_class {\\n   function test() {\\n     if (is_callable(array($this,'test_func1'))) {\\n         $this->test_func1();\\n     } else {\\n       echo \\\"test_func1 isn't callable from child\\\\n\\\";\\n     }\\n     if (is_callable(array($this,'test_func2'))) {\\n         $this->test_func2();\\n     } else {\\n       echo \\\"test_func2 isn't callable from child\\\\n\\\";\\n     }\\n     if (is_callable(array('test_class','test_func3'))) {\\n         test_class::test_func3();\\n     } else {\\n       echo \\\"test_func3 isn't callable from child\\\\n\\\";\\n     }\\n     if (is_callable(array('test_class','test_func4'))) {\\n         test_class::test_func4();\\n     } else {\\n       echo \\\"test_func4 isn't callable from child\\\\n\\\";\\n     }\\n   }\\n}\\n\\n$object = new test_class;\\n$object->test();\\nif (is_callable(array($object,'test_func1'))) {\\n    $object->test_func1();\\n} else {\\n  echo \\\"test_func1 isn't callable from outside\\\\n\\\";\\n}\\nif (is_callable(array($object,'test_func2'))) {\\n    $object->test_func2();\\n} else {\\n  echo \\\"test_func2 isn't callable from outside\\\\n\\\";\\n}\\nif (is_callable(array('test_class','test_func3'))) {\\n  test_class::test_func3();\\n} else {\\n  echo \\\"test_func3 isn't callable from outside\\\\n\\\";\\n}\\nif (is_callable(array('test_class','test_func4'))) {\\n  test_class::test_func4();\\n} else {\\n  echo \\\"test_func4 isn't callable from outside\\\\n\\\";\\n}\\n$object = new foo();\\n$object->test();\\n?>\",\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:261:   \"Bug #29210 (Function is_callable does not support private and protected methods)\",\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:263:   \"test_func1\\ntest_func2\\ntest_func3\\ntest_func4\\ntest_func1 isn't callable from outside\\ntest_func2 isn't callable from outside\\ntest_func3 isn't callable from outside\\ntest_func4 isn't callable from outside\\ntest_func1 isn't callable from child\\ntest_func2\\ntest_func3 isn't callable from child\\ntest_func4\",\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:837:   \"<?php\\nclass A {\\n    public function __call($m, $a) {\\n        echo \\\"__call\\\\n\\\";\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nclass B extends A {\\n    public static function foo() {\\n        echo \\\"foo\\\\n\\\";\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nif (is_callable(array('B', 'foo'))) {\\n    call_user_func(array('B', 'foo'));\\n}\\nif (is_callable(array('A', 'foo'))) {\\n    call_user_func(array('A', 'foo'));\\n}\\n?>\",\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:838:   :title \"Bug #34678 (__call(), is_callable() and static methods)\",\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:996:   \"EHLO\\n\\nDeprecated: Use of \\\"self\\\" in callables is deprecated in %s on line %d\\nEHLO\\n\\nDeprecated: Use of \\\"parent\\\" in callables is deprecated in %s on line %d\\nEHLO\",\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:1321:   \"Deprecated: Use of \\\"self\\\" in callables is deprecated in %s on line %d\\nDone\\nCHECKPOINT\",\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:1560:   \"<?php\\nclass Test {\\n    static function foo() {}\\n}\\n\\nvar_dump(is_callable(\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"));\\nvar_dump(is_callable(\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\"));\\nvar_dump(is_callable(\\\"x\\\\\\\\\\\"));\\nvar_dump(is_callable(\\\"\\\\\\\\x\\\"));\\nvar_dump(is_callable(\\\"x\\\\\\\\x\\\"));\\nvar_dump(is_callable(\\\"x\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"));\\nvar_dump(is_callable(\\\"\\\\\\\\x\\\"));\\nvar_dump(is_callable(\\\"x\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\x\\\"));\\nvar_dump(is_callable(\\\"cd\\\"));\\nvar_dump(is_callable(\\\"Test\\\\\\\\\\\"));\\nvar_dump(is_callable(\\\"\\\\\\\\Test\\\"));\\nvar_dump(is_callable(\\\"\\\\\\\\Test\\\\\\\\\\\"));\\nvar_dump(is_callable(\\\"Test::foo\\\"));\\nvar_dump(is_callable(\\\"\\\\\\\\Test::foo\\\"));\\nvar_dump(is_callable(\\\"is_string\\\"));\\nvar_dump(is_callable(\\\"\\\\\\\\is_string\\\"));\\n?>\",\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:1562:   \"Bug #43651 (is_callable() with one or more nonconsecutive colons crashes)\",\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:1599:   \"Bug #44141 (private parent constructor callable through static function)\",\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:1683:   \"__call:\\nstring(3) \\\"ABC\\\"\\n__call:\\nstring(3) \\\"ABC\\\"\\n__call:\\nstring(3) \\\"xyz\\\"\\n__call:\\nstring(3) \\\"www\\\"\\n\\nDeprecated: Use of \\\"self\\\" in callables is deprecated in %s on line %d\\n__call:\\nstring(1) \\\"y\\\"\\n\\nDeprecated: Use of \\\"self\\\" in callables is deprecated in %s on line %d\\n__call:\\nstring(1) \\\"y\\\"\\nok\\n__callstatic:\\nstring(3) \\\"www\\\"\\ncall_user_func(): Argument #1 ($callback) must be a valid callback, cannot access \\\"self\\\" when no class scope is active\",\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:1692:   \"__call:\\nstring(3) \\\"ABC\\\"\\n__call:\\nstring(3) \\\"ABC\\\"\\n__call:\\nstring(3) \\\"xyz\\\"\\n__call:\\nstring(3) \\\"www\\\"\\n\\nDeprecated: Use of \\\"self\\\" in callables is deprecated in %s on line %d\\n__call:\\nstring(1) \\\"y\\\"\\n\\nDeprecated: Use of \\\"self\\\" in callables is deprecated in %s on line %d\\n__call:\\nstring(1) \\\"y\\\"\\nok\\ncall_user_func(): Argument #1 ($callback) must be a valid callback, class bar does not have a method \\\"www\\\"\\ncall_user_func(): Argument #1 ($callback) must be a valid callback, cannot access \\\"self\\\" when no class scope is active\",\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:2028:   \"<?php\\n\\nclass ParentClass { }\\n\\nclass ChildClass extends ParentClass {\\n    public function testIsCallable() {\\n        var_dump(is_callable(array($this, 'parent::testIsCallable')));\\n    }\\n    public function testIsCallable2() {\\n        var_dump(is_callable(array($this, 'static::testIsCallable2')));\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n$child = new ChildClass();\\n$child->testIsCallable();\\n$child->testIsCallable2();\\n\\n?>\",\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:2030:   \"Bug #48899 (is_callable returns true even if method does not exist in parent class) [original test with deprecated syntax]\",\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:2037:   \"<?php\\n\\nclass ParentClass { }\\n\\nclass ChildClass extends ParentClass {\\n    public function testIsCallable() {\\n        var_dump(is_callable(array('ParentClass', 'testIsCallable')));\\n    }\\n    public function testIsCallable2() {\\n        var_dump(is_callable(array('ChildClass', 'testIsCallable2')));\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n$child = new ChildClass();\\n$child->testIsCallable();\\n$child->testIsCallable2();\\n\\n?>\",\ntest/yang/php/antlr/phpt_selection.edn:2039:   \"Bug #48899 (is_callable returns true even if method does not